window.docContents[213].resolve({"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Conjunction":{"contents":"\n\nTo prove that P ∧ Q holds, we must present evidence for both\nP and Q.  Thus, it makes sense to define a proof object for\nP ∧ Q to consist of a pair of two proofs: one for P and\nanother one for Q. This leads to the following definition.namespace AndPlayground\nstructure And (P Q : Prop) : Prop where\n  intro ::\n  left : P\n  right : Q\nNow that we have pulled back the covers a bit further, we can\nreveal another fact: a structure is just an inductive type with\none constructor and whose fields have names. By default,\nthe constructor is called mk, which we can rename to <name>\nusing <name> ::, as in the example.We can also see that this definition of And matches core\nAnd exactly: a structure with constructor intro\nand fields left/right\n(except that core uses a and b instead of P Q).#print _root_.And\nstructure _root_.And (a b : Prop) : Prop\nnumber of parameters: 2\nfields:\n  _root_.And.left : a\n  _root_.And.right : b\nconstructor:\n  _root_.And.intro {a b : Prop} (left : a) (right : b) : a ∧ b\nNotice the similarity with the definition of the MyProd type,\ngiven in chapter Poly; the only difference is that MyProd takes\nType arguments, whereas And takes Prop arguments.#print MyProd\nstructure MyProd (α β : Type) : Type\nnumber of parameters: 2\nfields:\n  MyProd.fst : α\n  MyProd.snd : β\nconstructor:\n  MyProd.mk {α β : Type} (fst : α) (snd : β) : MyProd α β\nThis similarity should clarify why cases, obtain ⟨hp, hq⟩,\nand intro ⟨hp, hq⟩\npatterns can be used on a conjunctive hypothesis.  Case analysis\nallows us to consider all possible ways in which P ∧ Q was\nproved — here just one (the And.intro constructor).theorem proj1' (P Q : Prop) (hpq : And P Q) : P := by\n    cases hpq with\n    | intro hp _ => exact hp\ntheorem and_comm (P Q : Prop) : And P Q ↔ And Q P := by\n  constructor\n  · intro h\n    obtain ⟨hp, hq⟩ := h\n    constructor\n    · exact hq\n    · exact hp\n  · intro h\n    obtain ⟨hq, hp⟩ := h\n    constructor\n    · exact hp\n    · exact hq\nend AndPlayground\nThis shows why the inductive (structure) definition of And can be\nmanipulated by tactics as we've been doing.  We can also use it to\nbuild proofs directly, using pattern-matching.  For instance:theorem proj1'' (P Q : Prop) (hpq : P ∧ Q) : P :=\n  match hpq with\n  | And.intro hp _ => hp\n\ntheorem and_comm'_aux (P Q : Prop) (h : P ∧ Q) : Q ∧ P :=\n  match h with\n  | And.intro hp hq => And.intro hq hp\n\ntheorem and_comm' (P Q : Prop) : P ∧ Q ↔ Q ∧ P :=\n  Iff.intro (and_comm'_aux P Q) (and_comm'_aux Q P)\nHere, Iff.intro is doing exactly the same thing as And.intro;\nit builds the Iff structure with its two arguments.Construct a proof object for the following proposition.theorem conj_fact : ∀ (P Q R : Prop),\n    P ∧ Q → Q ∧ R → P ∧ R :=\n  sorry\n\n\nWhat is the type of this expression?fun P Q R (h1 : And P Q) (h2 : And Q R) =>\n  match h1, h2 with\n  | And.intro hp _, And.intro _ hr => And.intro hp hr\n(A) ∀ (P Q R : Prop), P ∧ Q → Q ∧ R → P ∧ R(B) ∀ (P Q R : Prop), Q ∧ P → R ∧ Q → P ∧ R(C) ∀ (P Q R : Prop), P ∧ R(D) ∀ (P Q R : Prop), P ∨ Q → Q ∨ R → P ∨ R(E) Not typeable#check (fun P Q R (h1 : And P Q) (h2 : And Q R) =>\n          match h1, h2 with\n          | And.intro hp _, And.intro _ hr =>\n            And.intro hp hr\n        : ∀ (P Q R : Prop), P ∧ Q → Q ∧ R → P ∧ R)\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence\u0009Logical Connectives as Inductive Types","header":"10.6.1. Conjunction","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Conjunction"},"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Disjunction":{"contents":"\n\nThe inductive definition of disjunction uses two constructors, one\nfor each side of the disjunction:namespace OrPlayground\ninductive Or (P Q : Prop) : Prop where\n  | inl : P → Or P Q\n  | inr : Q → Or P Q\nThis declaration explains the behavior of the cases tactic on\na disjunctive hypothesis, since the generated subgoals match the\nshape of the Or.inl and Or.inr constructors.Once again, we can also directly write proof objects for theorems\ninvolving Or, without resorting to tactics.theorem inj_l : ∀ (P Q : Prop), P → Or P Q :=\n  fun _ _ hp => Or.inl hp\n\ntheorem inj_l' (P Q : Prop) (hp : P) : Or P Q := by\n  show_proof\n    left\n    exact hp\n?_\n\nafter left:\n  Or.inl ?_\n\nafter exact hp:\n  Or.inl hp\ntheorem or_elim : ∀ (P Q R : Prop),\n    Or P Q → (P → R) → (Q → R) → R :=\n  fun _ _ _ hpq hpr hqr =>\n    match hpq with\n    | Or.inl hp => hpr hp\n    | Or.inr hq => hqr hq\n\ntheorem or_elim' (P Q R : Prop) (hpq : Or P Q)\n    (hpr : P → R) (hqr : Q → R) : R := by\n  cases hpq with\n  | inl hp =>\n    apply hpr\n    exact hp\n  | inr hq =>\n    apply hqr\n    exact hq\nend OrPlayground\nConstruct a proof object for the following proposition.theorem or_commut' : ∀ (P Q : Prop), P ∨ Q → Q ∨ P :=\n  sorry\n\n\nWhat is the type of this expression?fun P Q h =>\n  match h with\n  | Or.inl hp => @Or.inr Q P hp\n  | Or.inr hq => @Or.inl Q P hq\n(A) ∀ P Q H, Q ∨ P ∨ H(B) ∀ (P Q : Prop), P ∨ Q → P ∨ Q(C) ∀ P Q H, P ∨ Q → Q ∨ P → H(D) ∀ (P Q : Prop), P ∨ Q → Q ∨ P(E) Not typeable#check (fun P Q h =>\n          match h with\n          | Or.inl hp => @Or.inr Q P hp\n          | Or.inr hq => @Or.inl Q P hq\n        : ∀ (P Q : Prop), P ∨ Q → Q ∨ P)\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009ProofObjects: The Curry-Howard Correspondence\u0009Logical Connectives as Inductive Types","header":"10.6.2. Disjunction","id":"/ProofObjects/#Logical-Foundations--ProofObjects___-The-Curry-Howard-Correspondence--Logical-Connectives-as-Inductive-Types--Disjunction"}});